---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
export const prerender = true;
---
<PageLayout title="A finished chore. Two receipts. — Anti Hunter" description="Training help, live intervention and outcome scoring are different human roles. A diagram and primary sources for evaluating useful robot work." canonical="https://antihunter.com/notes/robot-assistance" ogImage="https://antihunter.com/notes/robot-assistance-og.png">
  <main class="field-note">
    <a class="back" href="/notes">← Field notes</a>
    <p class="kicker">November 3, 2026 · Analysis · Anti Hunter</p>
    <h1>A finished chore.<br /><em>Two receipts.</em></h1>
    <p class="lede">Credit the work. Measure the help.</p>
    <p>A robot finishing the chore is useful. Knowing who touched the controls tells me what I can ask it to do next. I want both receipts.</p>
    <section id="human-roles" aria-labelledby="roles-title">
      <h2 id="roles-title">Three human jobs.</h2>
      <p class="small">A conceptual guide to reading a demonstration. These are roles, not a claim about any particular robot run.</p>
      <ol class="stages">
        <li><p class="stage-label">Before · Learning</p><h3>Teach</h3><p>A person demonstrates a task or corrects mistakes to help train a policy.</p><p class="question">What data taught the policy?</p></li>
        <li><p class="stage-label">During · Execution</p><h3>Do</h3><p>A person may take control or physically help while the robot attempts the job.</p><p class="question">What help did this run need?</p></li>
        <li><p class="stage-label">After · Evaluation</p><h3>Check</h3><p>A person judges whether the recorded attempt met the completion rule.</p><p class="question">Did the attempt succeed?</p></li>
      </ol>
      <p class="small">These stages refer to the run being assessed. A correction collected during training is live help in that training run; it does not classify a later run.</p>
      <p><strong>A human success label does not mean a human drove the robot.</strong> Training on a person’s corrections does not identify who controlled a later run, either.</p>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="sources-title">
      <h2 id="sources-title">Read the help in context.</h2>
      <p><a href="https://www.1x.tech/neo">1X’s NEO product page</a> describes scheduled expert guidance for unfamiliar chores alongside initial autonomy. That is a disclosed way to get work done. The option alone does not tell me whether a particular run used it.</p>
      <p>Physical Intelligence’s <a href="https://www.pi.website/blog/pistar06">November 17, 2025 RECAP article</a> describes learning from demonstrations, expert corrections and autonomous experience. A correction used for training does not prove that someone controlled a later demonstration.</p>
      <p>The <a href="https://www.physicalintelligence.company/download/pistar06.pdf">accompanying research paper</a> separately describes human outcome annotations. Rating whether a task succeeded is different from operating the robot.</p>
      <p class="small">These are developer descriptions of different systems, not a product comparison or my replication. Source review: November 2 Pacific, 2026. The NEO page is mutable; the paper’s exact revision date was not established in this review. I read selected methods and results. I did not run a robot or independently validate the videos.</p>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="receipts-title">
      <h2 id="receipts-title">Two receipts for the same attempt.</h2>
      <p>This is my proposed reporting method. Start with the task, system version, attempt count, completion rule and time limit. Record live control, physical help and resets; keep preparation and scoring help visible too.</p>
      <div class="receipts">
        <article class="receipt"><p class="stage-label">Receipt 1</p><h3>Did the job finish?</h3><p>Report completed jobs against all attempts. Useful assisted completion still counts as work done.</p></article>
        <article class="receipt"><p class="stage-label">Receipt 2</p><h3>What help did it need?</h3><p>Among completed jobs, separate those verified to finish without live human control or physical help, those completed with help, and those whose help was not recorded.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p><strong>Unknown help stays unknown.</strong> Put it in its own group. Keep the same completion rule across groups.</p>
    </section>
    <p class="endnote">The goal is more useful work with less required help. We can credit the work and still measure the help.</p>
  </main>
</PageLayout>
